nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $5 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=1.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/39/39d20dd045c97cae079f3839b4db071bd6b3b9ff133299ed4ae4d9421a55fee8.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{matrix}\\right| \\\\ &=\\left|\\begin{matrix}5&-2\\\\1&1\\end{matrix}\\right| \\\\ &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(7)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{7}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{1}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}[v]_{u^{2}}^{1}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}(1)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}(1 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + u]_{-1}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}(((1)+\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right))-((-1)+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e60f5e8-67d9-5947-9c0b-02d3e2848b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558874} {:answer/id #uuid "623d947d-bfd7-5e1b-be6c-685935878d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558880} {:answer/id #uuid "838009ad-af94-5c1e-9dc6-233cad98d542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558885} {:answer/id #uuid "d1051dd5-4fb6-5657-bf68-8d1d9e1a8de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558908} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d2af79-5742-5740-a01d-6a65765b9184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e60f5e8-67d9-5947-9c0b-02d3e2848b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558874} :answer-field/id #uuid "24456fc9-3202-52e6-96c1-45748de19c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0921561-14dc-5c7d-b935-87b9062309fa" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=9.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $8 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=9.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} \\\\ v=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,9).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18c4c61a55dd2a361b82a208eb9a0d26f3fcfa213fe83b05f23644c9f79becaa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{matrix}\\right| \\\\ &=\\left|\\begin{matrix}8&-2\\\\1&1\\end{matrix}\\right| \\\\ &=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(10)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{10}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{9}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}[v]_{u^{2}}^{9}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}(9)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}(9 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 9 u]_{-3}^{3} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(((27)+(-9))-((-27)+(9))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(36) \\\\ &=\\frac{18}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067912ba-31ed-5eca-bf1b-2e1a722add95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558853} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd8c0ca-63f1-573c-9fc6-3d6c2d297b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558879} {:answer/id #uuid "80b482c2-0595-595b-b947-9b4fc50000dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 32 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558884} {:answer/id #uuid "865bc14e-79fe-5274-a9ab-657177e6cb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558886} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebee877-3b12-5791-b4d8-5dd7a7a5887b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "067912ba-31ed-5eca-bf1b-2e1a722add95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558853} :answer-field/id #uuid "3313f71a-a789-5b76-a642-c2288a3b5693" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fcb07a25-db40-5466-b856-2310cb940e0e" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=4$ and the line $y=8.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 4 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=4\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=4\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2} - 4.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v=u^{2} - 4 \\\\ v=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2 \\sqrt{3},8).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,- 2 \\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le 2 \\sqrt{3},\\,u^{2} - 4\\le v\\le 8\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/23/2369c3299b61f996c6203e1e45a7a62c74cf9e8f4691c2d0941bdeec0a5ae53c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\left|\\begin{matrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{matrix}\\right| \\\\ &=\\left|\\begin{matrix}1&-4\\\\0&1\\end{matrix}\\right| \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}[\\int_{u^{2} - 4}^{8}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}[v]_{u^{2} - 4}^{8}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(8)-(u^{2} - 4)du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(12 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 12 u]_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &=1(((24 \\sqrt{3})+(- 8 \\sqrt{3}))-((- 24 \\sqrt{3})+(8 \\sqrt{3}))) \\\\ &=1(32 \\sqrt{3}) \\\\ &=32 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2c01b0-418c-5e7b-bef1-9f74e8360551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{s}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559041} {:answer/id #uuid "32f41347-0e90-5f70-a725-afb601591ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559143} {:answer/id #uuid "4b47a822-0376-58c7-bf52-c9886690a3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559215} {:answer/id #uuid "8260a8d8-6aad-5a51-b1f6-e47075b8707a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559340} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e51680-d23b-5e4b-b480-4740a65ffc07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32f41347-0e90-5f70-a725-afb601591ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559143} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d2a0144-e2db-559d-95d4-b901779d3f75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1fe48b5d-2d75-5bc6-88de-325b6398cfcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-45925" :question/problem "For an object moving in a straight line, its acceleration is the rate of change of its speed. What are the units of acceleration in terms of meters and seconds?" :question/worked-solution "We *measure the change* in the speed, as *we change* the time. Consequently, any appropriate unit must take the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{unit of speed}}{\\text{unit of time}}.\n$$\n\nIn terms of meters and seconds, the unit for speed is $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}},$ and the unit for time is $\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the appropriate unit for acceleration is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\right)}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nor, meters per square second."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd9f3f04-dec2-59a4-90c7-4e80640a9ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd9f3f04-dec2-59a4-90c7-4e80640a9ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559638} :answer-field/id #uuid "41fc7962-a273-588e-89ef-1ba97b8bff24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35d621eb-cf33-51a5-9f2c-6f01d51e0a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-347273" :question/problem "![](images/12/12a9961800e0578de92b428e1a1a6ba5e10e25fe635a90f85c772f9bf73dc997.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $15\\,cm\\times 7\\,cm.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{cm}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height $y$ of the box that gives the maximum possible volume.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=7-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $7-2y>0$, hence $y<\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(7-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-44{y}^{2}+105y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-88y+105,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-88y+105 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{35}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{35}{6}$ since we already know that $0<y<\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{3}{2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-88.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{3}{2}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the height of the box with the maximum volume is $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\text{ cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3088ae4-d68a-57e8-b0d8-0c5b606e66de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "648" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3088ae4-d68a-57e8-b0d8-0c5b606e66de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "648" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559463} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d8dbeb-a278-5ecd-a7ab-cdc1d19cdc10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b6389e0-fab8-5e1d-bb5a-1e8d075edcaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-346784" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4cafc2891cfd957aad10e876d51757e8c6d7e84844927cb57ce1c2e704fe5b55.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is three times its width. Given that the box's volume is $972$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $972$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 972 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=3x,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n972=3{x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y & =972 \\\\ y & =\\frac{324}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(3x)+2x\\left(\\frac{324}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{324}{{x}^{2}}\\right)(3x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+\\frac{648}{x}+\\frac{1944}{x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+\\frac{2592}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=12x-\\frac{2592}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12x-\\frac{2592}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{3}-2592 & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{3} & =2592 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =216 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=6$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=12+\\frac{5184}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(6)=12+24=36>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(6)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=6$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(6) & =6(6)^{2}+\\frac{2592}{6} \\\\  & =648\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016050f4-b327-51eb-ac59-6cdb4f2d394b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559017} {:answer/id #uuid "1649ec6b-8d70-5b6e-8662-99e140405802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559064} {:answer/id #uuid "80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559338} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a088d6-83f2-526a-b6ad-dd5f6c749f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559492} {:answer/id #uuid "cc39eb67-2c40-56a3-bfba-b0087f7d23c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80f25a70-a063-50e7-b4ec-df4df125ef08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559338} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e9c46f9-73fa-5bae-8ba5-d402d0aa2e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50aaea5b-0826-518a-8a27-2b0395848ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73789" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x-2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(3x+2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) & =3{\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3}+2{\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}-3\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)-2 \\\\  & =3\\left(-\\frac{8}{27}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9}+\\frac{8}{9}+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "365f8bbd-ce03-569d-aa3f-4ca177bcd8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z>0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559153} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2420ba-4165-5c8f-9927-4bbc2b816817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559172} {:answer/id #uuid "7240289b-9821-502d-a55b-8731f37e7d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559300} {:answer/id #uuid "93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559383} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb1c6fb-2082-5cdc-b6d2-731c9c2a61b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z<0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559383} :answer-field/id #uuid "115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" :question/math-academy-id "q-132637" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y+z},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when $y+z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y+z}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(y+z)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{2} & \\ne 0 \\\\ y+z & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198bc42c-d3a2-5588-8d98-878c5a5390fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559074} {:answer/id #uuid "2641419a-4016-5b04-ae0d-2222660b4187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559111} {:answer/id #uuid "51e3c066-4569-5d03-9a91-b2ea8579daae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559223} {:answer/id #uuid "576eba6e-2ba8-56c8-a2cb-7dfa5b572111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559240} {:answer/id #uuid "edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559591} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72017" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(2,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(2,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(2,-1)=\\frac{2}{(-1)}=-2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-1) & =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\frac{0}{1}=0,\n$$\n\n  and  ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(0,1)=\\frac{-0}{{1}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec0f707-e54c-51dd-acbe-ce56f922c04b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559135} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae0216e-3dda-5b86-bf9a-7a2f1941c30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\ge 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559279} {:answer/id #uuid "928531e1-339e-5d41-9d09-292c01dfe676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\le 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559380} {:answer/id #uuid "a464505c-7c18-5824-af43-db6d78d3830b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559429} {:answer/id #uuid "c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559505} :answer-field/id #uuid "13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72028" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}}\\right)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=-\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $h$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}}\\right)$ is defined only when $xz>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $h$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{h}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${h}_{x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{y}=-\\frac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $h$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cap \\,\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26d46271-3014-5476-b936-8ff5de06d051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26d46271-3014-5476-b936-8ff5de06d051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559112} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae05d3c-21d9-5363-8872-60ad0b3f9a59" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88d64cef-5be0-59d0-8f76-951d4350e51b" :question/math-academy-id "q-347083" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d67ba70d92807f0a7298fb8222252a047f7eec03a26878a5aa95b0fb82a7335c.png)\n\nA box with no lid whose depth $z$ is $3$ times its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$2$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $972\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $972\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 972 & =x\\cdot z\\cdot \\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right) \\\\ 972 & =\\frac{1}{3}x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x{z}^{2} & =972 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2\\,\\left(\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}}\\right)\\,z+2\\,\\left(\\frac{2916}{{z}^{2}}\\right)\\,\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)+2\\,\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)\\,z \\\\  & =\\frac{5832}{z}+\\frac{1944}{z}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7776}{z}+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=\\frac{4}{3}z-\\frac{7776}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}z-\\frac{7776}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{z}^{3} & =7776 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =5832 \\\\ z & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=18$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{15552}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(18)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(18)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=18$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=18$ units."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y):y>{x}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559062} {:answer/id #uuid "94fa791e-d489-52b8-9318-5172cd32b632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559385} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2ab564-0469-5d5f-bd5b-92ae71f9cfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{x}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559448} {:answer/id #uuid "f1bbb24c-f728-542a-b6fd-83644bef18b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y<{x}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559606} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c96cca-e498-5185-8483-99a97501e744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y):y>{x}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559062} :answer-field/id #uuid "d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" :question/math-academy-id "q-36525" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2x}{2(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70077891-86db-502d-895f-ded2194be2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559293} {:answer/id #uuid "72e91449-71dc-56a0-be51-67be87f1ed56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559304} {:answer/id #uuid "73e92523-fc3e-578c-9d40-6df5fc8e63ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=1+{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559306} {:answer/id #uuid "b3782bf8-ceaf-5b27-82d4-96d98b8fc40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559465} {:answer/id #uuid "b595f1c4-cac6-5c27-9491-cf38def6d400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b595f1c4-cac6-5c27-9491-cf38def6d400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559470} :answer-field/id #uuid "27573e10-1873-5b44-a68a-7f1ad53ececa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b49e8f9b-353c-5ece-9748-47abc8dc5438" :question/math-academy-id "q-5437" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n1,\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{9}{4},\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=1$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2},$ so we can find the common ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{1}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{3}{2},$  we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a01771-cde1-54b6-903b-5be07bb31b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559222} {:answer/id #uuid "7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559298} {:answer/id #uuid "7b90dcb3-20f2-5889-885c-583181c9f4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559323} {:answer/id #uuid "a0014fe0-63da-51e9-bf6d-ad45b394652b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559421} {:answer/id #uuid "fedcbdd6-f4a6-5750-a08c-d1f7b5c28221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7215867e-586e-537b-aa3c-c7a87d387e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559298} :answer-field/id #uuid "8508e80e-2237-5e61-952e-da76d1a6dd3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cef78ed2-9af9-556a-8a9d-05a7101331ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-112468" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(2x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(2x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(2x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & ={\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}-3\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{3}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(2x+1)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559996} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6f9c08-fc7c-5686-90ec-bd5e19971270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560003} {:answer/id #uuid "9107f827-2e1b-5ff9-a378-2a6c0345e47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560023} {:answer/id #uuid "bf504992-846a-53c0-b739-ef1c87c02b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560036} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ff7344-e51a-5da3-99bd-f7f28794aacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559996} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d783c00-550c-539f-9270-98d59cd31448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "141fe36a-507a-57e3-9528-7652085c824c" :question/problem "![](images/01/013cdd1ba73ee79ef51d00b7dc9f48488cee2299eb593accadfbf026981169c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{32 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5106df-61b9-5b24-863c-2ae5e567f591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559989} {:answer/id #uuid "2417f5d3-5389-5044-b2a0-418aeb7dac86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559997} {:answer/id #uuid "58f3e230-b70f-57b4-82bf-17794d911a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560008} {:answer/id #uuid "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560009} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa96423-8207-5f28-adff-de3f6375c04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560009} :answer-field/id #uuid "92fd1ae2-283e-5689-8e46-7ea5679c7ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c380e3c-eb26-50bd-a669-b181f14f84c3" :question/problem "![](images/79/79b09a881e6809e097e982b58d81a037d162f6b5820894e172955df1551415ca.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14cdfb2d-5fc7-5150-b284-439e38260d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559994} {:answer/id #uuid "4676e0a2-75d8-5a17-a969-7c99e3fa2152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560004} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac954c9-a0ef-5924-b0d7-d5aac29af9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560018} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbb113c-f187-55e0-8895-4843d66c32f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560049} {:answer/id #uuid "f6db0de2-0b22-5269-b5c3-7a0ea98a8477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3} \\left(2 - \\sqrt{2}\\right)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebbb113c-f187-55e0-8895-4843d66c32f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560049} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9f6651-4921-50c2-a3b2-e6a39b3ee3ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d921d71-b4a3-5244-8d78-9db05b10437e" :question/problem "![](images/13/137a452f1b378359fdd974b11781dddf1e7e9b469d864699b044eaf1cc1a7c20.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S y\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the first quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x - y)=1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(y)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(y)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\sin\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((0))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((0)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb0f77c-0203-5719-ab9d-91ce8ca040f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559995} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560026} {:answer/id #uuid "b35d874d-13e5-52c0-93cc-3d0c92d94055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560033} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4b7fd6-e628-5a99-898f-4ef27ca7dfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560040} {:answer/id #uuid "faaf8fd0-a189-5a52-a852-f75ff62ece6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560026} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e19b400-81aa-5f00-9d55-be29eb1d866d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "873a8289-cea9-556f-b99b-1f305a18b7fd" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbbb7c260754e6579bedc6e773656ea8defdb0493117f05e0e5f02fab4afb36c.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(1)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{1}{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =\\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\sqrt{3} \\pi$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f81263-c580-52d1-90e1-7fa8854069df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560010} {:answer/id #uuid "654cc51a-56c2-5bce-942c-7269e2a14dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560011} {:answer/id #uuid "8feb4f72-b6e7-5245-ba1e-f747a1cb18d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3} \\left(2 - \\sqrt{2}\\right)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560022} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe3dcf5-5100-544e-ae55-56e98c4a35fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560041} {:answer/id #uuid "fe74912e-c4ea-527e-af0a-bd1df787f279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbe3dcf5-5100-544e-ae55-56e98c4a35fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560041} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a717221-6128-50c0-937b-dab9d64bde09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2b56f89-2065-56e0-ba13-8da185fac240" :question/problem "![](images/81/819f039dfc5397bf393de37bbb17f4cb8888ce53a5370bfdf13d172697ffd2f7.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 y\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the first quadrant of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x - y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 y)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 y)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\sin\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((0))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((0)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d10bd8a-7e2d-5f7b-bfe7-2e0ae92a80cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}(t e^{2 t} + 4)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560138} {:answer/id #uuid "700dd878-159e-5841-92ce-18dd055b193b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}(- 8 \\sqrt{t} - t e^{t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560165} {:answer/id #uuid "aff49623-520d-5050-8c10-3f273f7a0cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}(- t e^{2 t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560192} {:answer/id #uuid "e5dda080-6f9e-5996-b6a9-a5b1df511468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}(-4)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560211} {:answer/id #uuid "f85ad87c-05c3-5572-b780-a25b41e3926b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}(- t e^{2 t} - 4)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f85ad87c-05c3-5572-b780-a25b41e3926b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{2}(- t e^{2 t} - 4)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560216} :answer-field/id #uuid "7533086f-6308-548a-9bbb-fda3d2b21e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7890e094-fef5-567c-b6e4-965fdc5b93aa" :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- x y^{2})\\,i+(- 8 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(e^{t})\\,i+(\\sqrt{t})\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,2]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=e^{t},\\,y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- x y^{2})\\,i+(- 8 y)\\,j \\\\  & =(- (e^{t}) (\\sqrt{t})^{2})\\,i+(- 8 (\\sqrt{t}))\\,j \\\\  & =(- t e^{t})\\,i+(- 8 \\sqrt{t})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{t})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =(e^{t})\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((- t e^{t})\\,i+(- 8 \\sqrt{t})\\,j)\\cdot ((e^{t})\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- t e^{t})\\cdot (e^{t})+(- 8 \\sqrt{t})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right) \\\\  & =- t e^{2 t} - 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{1}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{2}(- t e^{2 t} - 4)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dbb8114-0022-545b-b749-b3e641678284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}(t e^{2 t} + 1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560149} {:answer/id #uuid "5243f7e4-74ef-5df2-9100-845d622c7a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}(- t e^{2 t} - 1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560153} {:answer/id #uuid "abe385ed-8d08-52ec-ae91-225fba4548b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}(2 \\sqrt{t} + t e^{t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560190} {:answer/id #uuid "ba644c3a-40bf-59f7-9a07-a986b9585af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}(1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560196} {:answer/id #uuid "cdca2a95-175e-5c86-b898-ea1220da8ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}(t e^{2 t})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dbb8114-0022-545b-b749-b3e641678284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}(t e^{2 t} + 1)\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560149} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9f3daab-6a8a-5ae4-ada6-b16d6d099a72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f547fa50-e9ed-52ea-ac31-f84758ccc7ba" :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x y^{2})\\,i+(2 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(e^{t})\\,i+(\\sqrt{t})\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=e^{t},\\,y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x y^{2})\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\  & =((e^{t}) (\\sqrt{t})^{2})\\,i+(2 (\\sqrt{t}))\\,j \\\\  & =(t e^{t})\\,i+(2 \\sqrt{t})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{t})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =(e^{t})\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((t e^{t})\\,i+(2 \\sqrt{t})\\,j)\\cdot ((e^{t})\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\,j) \\\\  & =(t e^{t})\\cdot (e^{t})+(2 \\sqrt{t})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right) \\\\  & =t e^{2 t} + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{1}^{3}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}(t e^{2 t} + 1)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1974496f-78dd-5c57-b8ec-dc05525accf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560259} {:answer/id #uuid "31f0a8fb-fb91-5f05-a1c6-0f7919bcc8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560265} {:answer/id #uuid "61d1bf7e-2e8a-58b1-89e7-37532ce1d411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560275} {:answer/id #uuid "c650800f-fc8f-5c0f-87af-deec9d5ecc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560284} {:answer/id #uuid "ca25a9be-e802-5e45-a463-cc3c224d6a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca25a9be-e802-5e45-a463-cc3c224d6a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560286} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4dd3c96-722f-59d8-a3ad-81a44cf6e35a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c63a6c19-50b5-5979-ba7a-341fdd52675a" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=x \\sec{(y )}+y x^{4},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(- \\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x \\sec{(y )}+y x^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x \\sec{(y )})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y x^{4}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sec{(y )})+x^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (\\sec{(y )} \\tan{(y )})+x^{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x \\sec{(y )} \\tan{(y )}+x^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(- \\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi}{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(- \\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi}{4}) & =(- \\sqrt{2}) \\sec{(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) )} \\tan{(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) )}+(- \\sqrt{2})^{4} \\\\  & =(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (1)+(4) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca374ed-64c4-537e-9df7-38e602f55fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560463} {:answer/id #uuid "4301ea6f-1c49-5f63-a937-a2c2069919c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)={2}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560514} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e66bc-7a14-5aba-9a6a-a17f030fcf54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)={2}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560527} {:answer/id #uuid "679a841f-b122-5f85-a654-2b11ecc33553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{1}{{4}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560581} {:answer/id #uuid "955d3208-98c9-5cd1-a334-320a252d84ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ca374ed-64c4-537e-9df7-38e602f55fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560463} :answer-field/id #uuid "9947e7a8-c30a-59fb-977d-989052168899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "072376b1-6413-51af-98f6-95587e3acbf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-44986" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{2},\\,\\,f(1)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{-1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039ad15f-aa14-5833-b492-297d15ec8481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560368} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb8b6d5-6d81-50d2-85a0-0755a8afe296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560593} {:answer/id #uuid "897b3115-f689-53ea-8b8f-579dc209df81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-2\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560648} {:answer/id #uuid "89d2e657-a6a8-59f6-8532-79d2b8c65bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{50}{9}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560650} {:answer/id #uuid "af390c1b-5127-52ed-92c3-72c04d0ab64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=2\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89d2e657-a6a8-59f6-8532-79d2b8c65bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{50}{9}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560650} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee0f6b8-65be-5837-90b0-536ef0f796f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2e69b893-dca1-5a00-a0a1-d673b040380f" :question/math-academy-id "q-44983" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n}}{5},\\,\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{10}{3},\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{10}{3}.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=\\frac{10}{3}$ and $r=-\\frac{3}{5}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{-1}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right)\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10\\cdot 5}{3\\cdot 3}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{50}{9}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{50}{9}\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332e1d80-7be9-5c06-af32-c685cf22b488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560478} {:answer/id #uuid "66edb060-6c48-5d86-bc94-ca07b3f6eff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560580} {:answer/id #uuid "7014d6a2-28c0-585c-9c3a-7254efaca4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560602} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbe7726-805f-5ecb-9261-1dd23f6ff79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560682} {:answer/id #uuid "c24bd33a-9854-52b8-8040-811faade6cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66edb060-6c48-5d86-bc94-ca07b3f6eff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560580} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e3ba704-088a-57fb-bc54-70440ac77e83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "723435c8-f6c2-5011-8005-6ac70ede7884" :question/math-academy-id "q-90983" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-5(x-1)(x-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-5<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{1+2}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5(x-1)(x-2) \\\\  & =-5(\\frac{3}{2}-1)(\\frac{3}{2}-2) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{4}),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}].$\n\n![](images/42/426a8277921ca2c8282567eeb076c3dcf1f0f1c729c4ebd2e5d624f51b78d61e.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5b03d8-7fa4-5401-9f31-c0c170006fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560454} {:answer/id #uuid "480c4aef-5346-56fa-b6d8-38a9e3d58c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560522} {:answer/id #uuid "65fc6d60-ff84-513b-be77-831a85519a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560577} {:answer/id #uuid "9b81d004-13c7-55f1-aef5-1fbc7d19388a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560681} {:answer/id #uuid "d99e13f9-a196-576c-9387-f6d7991d38e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d99e13f9-a196-576c-9387-f6d7991d38e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560792} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f775890-b185-5534-961a-edb8b2135025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e89138a9-d6b8-5061-b7fa-8f7c0010f6f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-341218" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi /3$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=f(0+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(0)=F(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=f(0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fffa964-0c5d-5367-882a-37b3c5037713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=\\frac{49}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561461} {:answer/id #uuid "24cb3f01-9a76-506d-ab3d-42942c2d3586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=\\frac{49}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561466} {:answer/id #uuid "59dc7adf-29fb-58bf-bfc7-aa685892268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561478} {:answer/id #uuid "c7113c23-1067-5a12-ab61-0e4785025a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=\\frac{49}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561514} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e7bc64-654c-5b65-ae5b-4883088dc630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=\\frac{49}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7113c23-1067-5a12-ab61-0e4785025a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=\\frac{49}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561514} :answer-field/id #uuid "83292d95-cf93-5acc-8655-45bf8bc97864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06e629ab-c275-55a6-813f-9310bf8a4b4b" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2aba916c2a8e1513a9d1f79bbb6820559e188d3166ba9d4323cce018491f74d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(3,4)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{7}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31b4e07c-43c7-5b7c-86ee-bbfbee73b3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{196}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561470} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7b0fb9-b54d-5dce-b02b-884703b0e99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=\\frac{196}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561492} {:answer/id #uuid "82c8d150-23b7-54fa-8871-76b7ff7365f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=\\frac{196}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561496} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6ad6ce-84a0-5d0e-9f6b-72cac338cc26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{196}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561506} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e6952d-91f1-517e-8c30-5095b978f727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7b0fb9-b54d-5dce-b02b-884703b0e99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=\\frac{196}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561492} :answer-field/id #uuid "220fac2c-89c5-51d1-aa4d-d4ae66fbb1b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d65a625-1ad4-5ef2-8b76-a65fd658c912" :question/problem "![](images/18/1893e7f4fa7b6b3927a3b3750f526a747b4be615f91a60a446231df0b4bf8a91.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(2,3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{14}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =\\frac{196}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75abbf65-1902-5344-828d-f2979f123894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{23}{4})^{2}=\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561486} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3e6896-9408-5411-9efa-d8cb0f877d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y-\\frac{23}{4})^{2}=\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561500} {:answer/id #uuid "bbfd8876-cf93-555f-b033-970f615348ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{23}{4})^{2}=\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561510} {:answer/id #uuid "c27c5584-8c61-5764-ac6b-4eab0311c534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{23}{4})^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561511} {:answer/id #uuid "c56910fc-16e8-506e-85cc-9531fbee8443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y-\\frac{23}{4})^{2}=\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75abbf65-1902-5344-828d-f2979f123894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{23}{4})^{2}=\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561486} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f52003e-bd4f-5623-b138-faecec84d950" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "222f72e4-d050-5b85-9aef-449a71fa71d0" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8da1de86395af2b85313ba5fafe11d46aac395451f06a5820726eeabf71b8029.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(\\frac{17}{4},- \\frac{23}{4})$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{23}{4}\\right))^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{23}{4})^{2} & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a758ae-c676-50d8-ac13-a34450f51872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{9}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{13}{3})^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561459} {:answer/id #uuid "3118e63d-0efd-5f33-b357-ad70213a55a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+\\frac{9}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{13}{3})^{2}=\\frac{64}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561469} {:answer/id #uuid "37c7025c-a036-5201-af73-abaabfe454ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{9}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{13}{3})^{2}=\\frac{64}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561472} {:answer/id #uuid "840a60b6-ea5c-5978-855e-48db37c05f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-\\frac{9}{4})^{2}+(y-\\frac{13}{3})^{2}=\\frac{64}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561498} {:answer/id #uuid "e35dda4f-3dc6-